\ ff-test: lines
\ Edge cases that read out of bounds, ran undefined behaviour, or went
\ quietly wrong. Each section says what used to happen.

: p 32 emit . ;

\ `pick` and `roll` took their index as an int: a huge one read far
\ outside the stack, one past 2^32 wrapped to a small index, and a `roll`
\ reaching too deep wrote its index over the item below it.
1 2 3 2147483647 pick
clear
1 2 3 4294967297 pick
clear
1 2 3 4294967298 roll
clear
1 2 3 5 roll
depth p p p p p cr
1 2 3 2 pick p cr
clear
1 2 3 2 roll p p p cr

\ `parse` consumed the input's terminator when the delimiter was 0 (or
\ 256), and the next read ran on past the end of the input.
: rest 0 parse type ;
: rest-256 256 parse type ;
rest of the line
cr
rest-256 runs to the end too
cr
41 parse group) type cr

\ `fix` of a real whose integer part doesn't fit a cell, or of NaN, was
\ undefined behaviour in C.
-2.5 fix p 2e9 fix p cr
1e300 fix
-1e300 fix
1e300 1e300 f* fix
1e300 1e300 f* dup f- fix
clear

\ `@`, `!`, `+!` and `?` used whatever address they got as a cell
\ pointer: undefined behaviour when it isn't cell-aligned — `c,` makes
\ that easy — and a fault on strict-alignment CPUs.
create buf 1 c, 0 , 0 ,
42 buf 1 + !
buf 1 + @ p
5 buf 1 + +!
32 emit buf 1 + ? cr

\ A `(` comment still open at the end of an `evaluate` string ran on
\ into the caller's input and the lines after it; a `\` ending a line of
\ a multi-line input commented out the next line too.
"( open" evaluate p 1 2 + p cr
7 p cr
"1 p \\\n2 p 3 p" evaluate drop cr

\ A scope that left the return stack unbalanced raised its error but
\ kept its barrier over the caller's cells: `depth` read 0, and `drop`
\ failed on cells that were still there.
: unbalanced { ( a -- ) 1 >r } ;
5 6 7 unbalanced
depth p drop drop drop depth p cr
